title: What counts as a duplicate
tags: Duplicates
aliases: hard-link inode
kind: reference
summary: Tracked files with identical content — no directories, symlinks, empty files or offline volumes — and how hard links are counted.

Two metarecords are duplicates when their files have identical content. The
candidates are deliberately narrow:

* ''Files only'' (`mfr_type = "file"`). Not directories — their "content" is their subtree, not bytes — and not symlinks, since hashing one reads through to its target; a symlink's content is its `mfr_symlink_target`, which compares directly.
* ''With a live `mfr_path`''. A metarecord whose file is gone has nothing to hash.
* ''Never empty files.'' They are all identical, which is true and useless: removing them frees nothing. `--min-size` raises the bar further.
* ''Not on an offline volume'' ([[Offline subtrees]]), and not unreadable (permission denied, a disconnected mount): both are counted as `skipped` in the summary, neither is an error.

The scan follows the ''tracked set'' — every metarecord of a file — not what is
watched: `mf_watch` and `mf_ignore` are not consulted ([[Watch and ignore fields]]). A subtree whose watching
was turned off still holds metarecords, and those files are still duplicates of
each other.

!! Hard links

Several paths can name the same inode: identical by construction, so
duplicates — but removing one frees nothing, and a tool that reports reclaimable
space without accounting for them lies.

The daemon records `mfr_inode` (`"<dev>:<ino>"`) on a file whose link count is
greater than one; its ''presence'' marks a hard-linked file. It comes from the
`stat` already done while walking, so it costs no extra system call, and it is
written for hard-linked files only. The device is part of the value because an
inode number is unique only within one filesystem, and a repository can span
several.

Within a group, members sharing one `mfr_inode` are one file under several
names, so the space a group wastes is counted over distinct inodes, a member
without `mfr_inode` counting as its own:

```
reclaimable = mfr_content_size × (distinct inodes − 1)
```

''Known staleness.'' Creating or removing a link fires a filesystem event for
''that'' path only: after `ln A B`, `B` has its `mfr_inode` and `A` none until
something re-stats it, so the group counts two distinct inodes and reclaimable
space is ''over''-reported — the error is on the side of promising space that
removing a name would not free, never of hiding a duplicate. A reconcile
corrects it.
